1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:07,800
They are the toughest ships in the water, plowing headlong into one of nature's hardest obstacles.

2
00:00:08,840 --> 00:00:19,120
They call the most desolate places on Earth home, as they keep ships safe and open the frozen reaches of the planet to scientific study.

3
00:00:22,540 --> 00:00:25,740
Now, Icebreakers on Modern Marvels.

4
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:40,440
Ice.

5
00:00:41,240 --> 00:00:43,900
One of nature's most formidable barriers.

6
00:00:46,640 --> 00:00:52,200
In the channels and shipping routes of the world, it can bring transportation to a dead stop.

7
00:00:55,120 --> 00:00:57,620
This is the adversary of the icebreaker.

8
00:01:00,000 --> 00:01:08,300
Specially designed ships with reinforced hulls and massive power to shatter winter's frozen domain.

9
00:01:12,500 --> 00:01:20,400
Wherever ice gets in the way of maritime commerce or scientific research, ice-breaking ships are called on to open the way.

10
00:01:23,700 --> 00:01:29,280
They do this by grooming channels, creating lanes through which ships follow.

11
00:01:30,000 --> 00:01:37,060
Other times they get up close and personal by winching the bow of a beset vessel into

12
00:01:37,061 --> 00:01:44,820
a specially designed padded notch and using the power of both ships to force through the

13
00:01:44,821 --> 00:01:54,980
frozen hardback.

14
00:01:54,981 --> 00:01:59,220
It is late January on the Great Lakes.

15
00:01:59,221 --> 00:02:06,220
A smooth, undisturbed layer of white covers the entire landscape.

16
00:02:08,360 --> 00:02:17,100
At this time of year it is difficult to tell where the shore ends and the water begins.

17
00:02:17,101 --> 00:02:21,860
On Lake Huron, the ice is usually only two feet thick.

18
00:02:21,861 --> 00:02:25,540
But the wind can cause the ice to pile up on itself,

19
00:02:25,541 --> 00:02:30,580
forming a pressure ridge many times thicker.

20
00:02:30,581 --> 00:02:34,980
Shipped goods are imperative for the Great Lakes communities.

21
00:02:34,981 --> 00:02:41,760
And ships carrying these goods must make their way through this ice.

22
00:02:41,761 --> 00:02:46,920
Detroit alone needs 65,000 tons of fuel oil every day,

23
00:02:46,921 --> 00:02:49,140
much of which will be transported by barge.

24
00:02:52,900 --> 00:02:56,960
The 290-foot U.S. Coast Guard cutter, Mackinac,

25
00:02:57,460 --> 00:03:00,060
has been charged with keeping these supply lines open.

26
00:03:02,320 --> 00:03:04,320
With her reinforced hull,

27
00:03:06,400 --> 00:03:07,600
spoon-shaped bow,

28
00:03:10,600 --> 00:03:15,040
and mighty 10,000-horsepower diesel-electric engines,

29
00:03:15,041 --> 00:03:19,800
the Mackinac has been designed for one purpose

30
00:03:19,801 --> 00:03:22,420
icebreaking

31
00:03:22,421 --> 00:03:31,440
the U.S. Coast Guard cutter Mackinac is a unique one-of-a-kind icebreaker

32
00:03:31,441 --> 00:03:35,240
it was built in 1944 during the war

33
00:03:35,241 --> 00:03:39,800
and it was built in a response to essentially help move the iron ore

34
00:03:39,801 --> 00:03:43,580
that was needed to do the ship construction for the war effort

35
00:03:43,581 --> 00:03:51,580
As the oldest icebreaker on the Great Lakes, her tenure has made her one of the most famous

36
00:03:51,581 --> 00:03:55,860
ships in these waters.

37
00:03:55,861 --> 00:04:01,860
In nearly 60 years of service, the Mackinac has seen many renovations.

38
00:04:01,861 --> 00:04:08,420
On the bridge, ornate 1940s original equipment share space with modern radar and navigation

39
00:04:08,421 --> 00:04:12,420
instruments.

40
00:04:12,421 --> 00:04:18,300
What makes her truly remarkable as an icebreaker is her combination of strength and power.

41
00:04:19,920 --> 00:04:28,600
Well, the Mackinac, like all icebreakers, have special features that allow them to take on the massive forces of the ice.

42
00:04:29,080 --> 00:04:35,500
One is the hull form, which most of the ships now have a kind of a spoon bow,

43
00:04:35,640 --> 00:04:39,740
and they look sort of like a football cut in half if you were to pull them out of the water.

44
00:04:39,741 --> 00:04:49,020
The unique hull shape allows the ship to ride up on top of an ice sheet, using the weight of the vessel to break through.

45
00:04:50,340 --> 00:04:56,680
Along with that hull form, they also need very thick hull plating where it meets the ice,

46
00:04:56,740 --> 00:05:01,660
and they also need a lot of structure behind the plating to reinforce against the ice.

47
00:05:03,000 --> 00:05:06,960
An intricate array of steel girders form the ribs of the Mackinac.

48
00:05:06,961 --> 00:05:14,080
Placed 16 inches apart, they support and reinforce the 2-inch steel hull plating,

49
00:05:14,900 --> 00:05:17,300
which is nearly twice the thickness of normal ships.

50
00:05:20,260 --> 00:05:22,860
The additional strength comes with a price.

51
00:05:24,100 --> 00:05:27,500
The Mackinac displaces over 5,000 tons fully loaded.

52
00:05:28,720 --> 00:05:33,620
Moving a ship this size through the ice requires an enormous amount of power.

53
00:05:33,621 --> 00:05:38,440
On board Mackinac, there are three main entrants.

54
00:05:39,180 --> 00:05:42,500
This is one of them. It is the heart and soul of the vessel.

55
00:05:43,520 --> 00:05:50,860
We have six main diesel engines, Fairbanks, Morris, the post piston, and they are coupled to a generator.

56
00:05:52,720 --> 00:05:58,160
The generators provide electricity for both of the 5,000 horsepower propulsion motors,

57
00:05:58,700 --> 00:06:03,520
which in turn spin the two 10.7 ton aft propellers.

58
00:06:03,620 --> 00:06:13,900
the diesel electric engine has been used on board icebreakers for quite a while and it's the preferred

59
00:06:13,901 --> 00:06:20,640
power plant primarily because when the propeller goes through the ice it hits a large piece there's

60
00:06:20,641 --> 00:06:26,460
a shock down that propeller and it's transmitted back through the shaft the propeller shafts are

61
00:06:26,461 --> 00:06:32,760
spun by the powerful electric motors which utilize magnetic fields for propulsion as opposed to being

62
00:06:32,761 --> 00:06:37,380
directly attached to the diesel power plant, which would require additional gears and mechanical

63
00:06:37,381 --> 00:06:41,560
linkages, more to damage if a propeller were suddenly jammed in the ice.

64
00:06:45,140 --> 00:06:48,280
The Mackinac has other traits that make her at home in the ice.

65
00:06:50,780 --> 00:06:55,912
Onboard tanks and pumps comprise a heel and trim system.

66
00:06:55,913 --> 00:06:59,120
In 30 seconds, over 100,000 gallons

67
00:06:59,121 --> 00:07:03,347
of water can be pumped from one side of the boat to the other.

68
00:07:03,348 --> 00:07:05,120
The rapid shift in ballast

69
00:07:05,121 --> 00:07:12,015
causes the over 74-foot wide ship to rock, crushing the surrounding ice.

70
00:07:12,016 --> 00:07:13,260
An additional

71
00:07:13,261 --> 00:07:19,680
propeller at the bow can also be employed. By turning the water ahead of the vessel,

72
00:07:19,681 --> 00:07:27,435
the propeller destabilizes the ice while washing the broken pieces from its path.

73
00:07:27,436 --> 00:07:28,680
But the years

74
00:07:28,681 --> 00:07:31,150
icebreaking have taken their toll.

75
00:07:31,151 --> 00:07:35,000
And the mighty Mackinac is beginning to show her age.

76
00:07:35,740 --> 00:07:40,099
Because of her age, it's costing a lot of money to keep the Mackinac operational.

77
00:07:40,100 --> 00:07:40,800
And

78
00:07:40,801 --> 00:07:46,560
it also takes a lot of people on board to do the maintenance. Plans right now are to

79
00:07:46,561 --> 00:07:51,840
replace this ship with a new icebreaker that'll be multi-mission in nature.

80
00:07:53,020 --> 00:07:57,540
But until the replacement is built, the Mackinac remains on patrol.

81
00:07:58,680 --> 00:08:02,580
As long as there is ice in the water, there is plenty of work to be done.

82
00:08:04,900 --> 00:08:09,040
Up to 60 merchant ships navigate the frozen waters of the Great Lakes,

83
00:08:09,540 --> 00:08:12,000
and the Mackinac cannot protect them all.

84
00:08:13,420 --> 00:08:15,680
Ten additional icebreakers share the duty.

85
00:08:16,380 --> 00:08:19,260
Two Canadian vessels, three buoy tenders,

86
00:08:19,800 --> 00:08:26,800
and five Bay-class icebreaking tugs work as a team to keep all the Great Lakes open to shipping.

87
00:08:28,680 --> 00:08:33,160
The U.S. Coast Guard also looks to the skies for help.

88
00:08:33,980 --> 00:08:38,200
Because when breaking ice, a straight line is not always the best route.

89
00:08:41,140 --> 00:08:45,920
The H-65 Dolphin helicopters lift ice advisors from the ships.

90
00:08:49,240 --> 00:08:53,700
Once a lot, they relay course information back to the captains.

91
00:08:53,701 --> 00:09:00,640
When you're on the bridge of the cutter, you can only see so far.

92
00:09:01,180 --> 00:09:06,660
Being able to climb to 1,000 to 2,000 feet, we can give them a better idea of where they should go to hit the open water.

93
00:09:07,080 --> 00:09:10,640
That would be a much less arduous task than breaking through all the hard stuff.

94
00:09:12,580 --> 00:09:14,260
They are our eyes in the sky.

95
00:09:14,261 --> 00:09:22,380
If we're working in a lake environment such as this, we could be working a mile or two miles away from a great open water lead and not nail it.

96
00:09:23,700 --> 00:09:31,760
But today, the air crew has spotted an enormous ice sheet,

97
00:09:32,440 --> 00:09:36,580
blocking the route onto the Mackinac Bridge at the northern tip of Lake Huron.

98
00:09:40,200 --> 00:09:45,960
The ice-breaking tug Katmai Bay has been called on to clear the potentially dangerous flow.

99
00:09:46,960 --> 00:09:50,100
If the ship was traveling through that area and happened to get stuck,

100
00:09:50,101 --> 00:09:54,620
it is possible that that sheet could break loose and start carrying the ship with it anywhere it goes.

101
00:09:55,240 --> 00:09:58,420
If the ship is stuck, it's at the mercy of nature, and the wind's blowing it.

102
00:09:58,421 --> 00:10:01,520
It could blow it onto a shoal or into some other hazard of navigation.

103
00:10:03,720 --> 00:10:08,900
The Bay-class tugs are the most technologically sophisticated icebreakers on the Great Lakes.

104
00:10:09,920 --> 00:10:19,300
But for this task, brute force and an experienced crew are all that is necessary.

105
00:10:20,100 --> 00:10:26,800
Sometimes we find a pressure ridge, a large windrow that we are unable to just break through on the first time.

106
00:10:26,880 --> 00:10:29,720
We have to employ a backing and ramming maneuver.

107
00:10:30,260 --> 00:10:33,720
And we'll take the ship and we'll back up in the track that we just established,

108
00:10:33,860 --> 00:10:37,220
anywhere from two to three ship lengths, and then we'll come ahead again.

109
00:10:37,221 --> 00:10:41,020
And we'll use the backing ability of the ship to keep it in as straight as line as possible.

110
00:10:41,021 --> 00:10:53,380
backing up on the ice can pose a problem for normal non ice breaking ships if a

111
00:10:53,381 --> 00:11:00,520
fragment were to jam the rudder the ship would turn out of control icebreakers

112
00:11:00,521 --> 00:11:06,340
like the cat my bay have rudders which are braced at both the top and bottom an

113
00:11:06,341 --> 00:11:11,200
An underwater protrusion called an ice horn shields the rudder and helps the ship break

114
00:11:11,201 --> 00:11:13,389
ice when moving backwards.

115
00:11:13,390 --> 00:11:18,860
Windblown ice like that found under the Mackinac Bridge can stack

116
00:11:18,861 --> 00:11:26,300
and fold over on itself, creating pressure ridges up to 20 feet thick. The ship will

117
00:11:26,301 --> 00:11:35,520
have to back and ram many times to get through this extremely tough ice.

118
00:11:35,521 --> 00:11:42,520
Unfortunately, the Bay-class ships have other weapons at their disposal.

119
00:11:44,420 --> 00:11:51,420
Hull lubrication, or bubbler systems, helps clear the ice from around the ships.

120
00:11:52,020 --> 00:11:56,120
Air is expelled through holes below the water line.

121
00:11:56,121 --> 00:12:03,120
The bubbles which are generated force their way up through the ice, causing it to weaken.

122
00:12:05,520 --> 00:12:10,860
Once the ship has broken through the pack ice, the specially designed bow and powerful

123
00:12:10,861 --> 00:12:16,540
crop wash create wave action in the surrounding water.

124
00:12:16,541 --> 00:12:23,900
The ripple effect causes the ice to flex and break apart.

125
00:12:23,901 --> 00:12:27,080
The Coast Guard needs these technological tricks.

126
00:12:27,081 --> 00:12:32,460
By the end of the season it has spent over 4,600 hours assisting more than 500 trapped

127
00:12:32,461 --> 00:12:38,700
ships, any one of which could have become a financial disaster, or worse, a human tragedy.

128
00:12:39,960 --> 00:12:43,600
But the icebreakers and their crews keep the waters safe.

129
00:12:44,420 --> 00:12:47,445
One of the things that makes this such a great ship is the crew.

130
00:12:47,446 --> 00:12:48,580
They're the ones that do

131
00:12:48,581 --> 00:12:53,680
all the hard work. Without a doubt, they're a bunch of can-do coasties.

132
00:12:56,880 --> 00:13:00,060
For icebreakers, the Great Lakes are a microcosm.

133
00:13:00,061 --> 00:13:05,060
This same scenario plays out with hundreds of ships all over the world.

134
00:13:05,061 --> 00:13:12,060
Where people in cold climates depend on shipping, icebreakers are there to clear the way.

135
00:13:16,060 --> 00:13:27,060
Next, the combination of economics and polar exploration force early shipbuilders and mariners to push the boundaries and risk their lives.

136
00:13:30,060 --> 00:13:36,940
The Cutter Mackinac's role in the Great Lakes was considered to be so important that she was designed to never leave.

137
00:13:37,580 --> 00:13:42,160
She was purposely built too large to pass through the existing locks leading to the ocean.

138
00:13:42,960 --> 00:13:44,080
Icebreakers will return.

139
00:13:48,460 --> 00:13:53,000
The icebreaker has changed the way ships navigate the waterways of the world.

140
00:13:55,300 --> 00:13:59,900
Modern icebreakers can smash through 10-foot-thick ice sheets without stopping.

141
00:14:00,060 --> 00:14:04,600
But for early wooden wind-powered ships,

142
00:14:04,800 --> 00:14:08,260
the best way to deal with ice was not to be in the water.

143
00:14:10,240 --> 00:14:11,880
Prior to icebreakers,

144
00:14:12,700 --> 00:14:16,000
the countries that had major ice problems

145
00:14:16,001 --> 00:14:19,020
would have to just stop all operations in that area.

146
00:14:19,360 --> 00:14:21,600
They would have to move their shipping out of the area

147
00:14:21,601 --> 00:14:24,340
just because they would be caught and crushed in the ice.

148
00:14:24,341 --> 00:14:32,380
Lightly built wind-powered ships were no match for a frozen waterway.

149
00:14:35,780 --> 00:14:41,680
That would begin to change with the perfection of the steam engine by James Watt in the late 1700s

150
00:14:41,681 --> 00:14:45,200
and the subsequent creation of steam-powered ships.

151
00:14:46,120 --> 00:14:48,260
But there were still problems.

152
00:14:48,440 --> 00:14:53,540
In the 1830s and 40s, when you begin to have steam-powered vessels,

153
00:14:53,541 --> 00:14:59,100
even they were too underpowered to punch their way through ice.

154
00:14:59,740 --> 00:15:02,180
Most of these vessels still had sail

155
00:15:02,181 --> 00:15:06,080
and even the combination of both were too underpowered.

156
00:15:08,740 --> 00:15:12,180
Captains who blundered into heavy ice had few options.

157
00:15:13,560 --> 00:15:15,520
Depending on the atmospheric conditions,

158
00:15:16,460 --> 00:15:18,520
the ships could be trapped until a thaw

159
00:15:18,521 --> 00:15:21,100
or mercilessly crushed.

160
00:15:23,540 --> 00:15:28,600
The crews would toil to free their ships with sledgehammers

161
00:15:28,601 --> 00:15:33,400
or anything else they could find to stave off the encroaching freeze.

162
00:15:36,740 --> 00:15:41,320
The other things they could do was put an anchor out on the ice

163
00:15:41,321 --> 00:15:44,740
and then use the ship's caps and winch themselves forward.

164
00:15:45,060 --> 00:15:47,420
So there really wasn't much you could do.

165
00:15:48,020 --> 00:15:51,960
And probably the best illustration of this is what happened to Ernest Shackleton

166
00:15:51,961 --> 00:15:54,260
and the Antarctic, his ship was trapped,

167
00:15:55,020 --> 00:15:56,140
drifted for many miles.

168
00:15:56,560 --> 00:15:58,320
Eventually, the ice crushed it.

169
00:16:01,620 --> 00:16:04,300
Still, many mariners remained undaunted,

170
00:16:05,160 --> 00:16:08,080
particularly those who stood to make a great deal of money

171
00:16:08,081 --> 00:16:12,260
hunting whales and seals near the frozen poles.

172
00:16:12,980 --> 00:16:15,340
In the 1870s or 1880s,

173
00:16:15,420 --> 00:16:18,020
whaling and sealing became a fairly good business.

174
00:16:18,460 --> 00:16:21,940
Therefore, we had to start getting more strengthened ships for this

175
00:16:21,941 --> 00:16:26,120
and they started to put better framing in the ships

176
00:16:26,121 --> 00:16:28,960
and strengthen them on the outside of the ship also.

177
00:16:30,420 --> 00:16:33,780
The United States' first real foray into icebreaking

178
00:16:33,781 --> 00:16:37,400
came with the purchase of the Alaska Territory in 1867.

179
00:16:39,200 --> 00:16:42,420
The federal government charged the Revenue Cutter Service,

180
00:16:42,720 --> 00:16:44,600
later to become the U.S. Coast Guard,

181
00:16:45,080 --> 00:16:46,660
with regular missions to the region.

182
00:16:47,580 --> 00:16:49,740
The ships of the Bering Sea Patrol

183
00:16:49,741 --> 00:16:51,960
would set sail in the spring from Seattle.

184
00:16:52,720 --> 00:16:54,200
Heading north as the ice melted,

185
00:16:54,820 --> 00:16:57,100
they would stop in native villages along the way.

186
00:16:58,680 --> 00:17:00,620
The captains of those early cutters

187
00:17:00,621 --> 00:17:03,240
were the only government representatives in the territory.

188
00:17:04,180 --> 00:17:05,960
Part diplomat and part law enforcement,

189
00:17:06,520 --> 00:17:09,200
they were both loved and feared by local inhabitants.

190
00:17:10,280 --> 00:17:12,340
And they had a doctor and a dentist aboard.

191
00:17:12,460 --> 00:17:13,620
They would take care of your ills.

192
00:17:13,621 --> 00:17:16,900
And also, the captain was usually appointed a federal judge,

193
00:17:17,020 --> 00:17:18,740
and he would sit down and hold trials.

194
00:17:19,740 --> 00:17:23,260
hanging from the yardarm or whatever, go to the next town.

195
00:17:25,820 --> 00:17:32,000
The captains and crews would also be responsible for enforcing some of America's earliest environmental laws.

196
00:17:32,380 --> 00:17:38,340
There was a lot of over-harvesting of seals at this point from not only the United States but from other nations,

197
00:17:38,440 --> 00:17:42,060
and they would send the cutters up there to help regulate this and protect the seals.

198
00:17:42,640 --> 00:17:46,340
One of the cutters in particular was the rush that was up there.

199
00:17:46,341 --> 00:17:47,880
She went up there for a number of years.

200
00:17:48,200 --> 00:17:52,700
And the old saying, get there to avoid the rush, comes from that.

201
00:17:52,800 --> 00:17:55,720
People that were going to do anything illegal had to get there before the rush arrived.

202
00:17:57,860 --> 00:18:01,140
Other ships would become renowned for their work in the Alaskan waters.

203
00:18:02,720 --> 00:18:06,200
Two of the vessels that the Revenue Cutter Service eventually gets,

204
00:18:06,380 --> 00:18:08,920
the Bayer and the Thetis, were both built as whalers.

205
00:18:09,560 --> 00:18:13,260
And, of course, whalers are designed to work in light ice

206
00:18:13,261 --> 00:18:15,920
because they can capture whales and kill them.

207
00:18:16,340 --> 00:18:21,880
Both of these vessels were built with heavy hulls and were reinforced to work in ice.

208
00:18:22,720 --> 00:18:25,000
Known as the Coast Guard's old ironsides,

209
00:18:25,600 --> 00:18:29,920
the 198-foot Bear was constructed in Dundee, Scotland.

210
00:18:30,520 --> 00:18:33,889
Built in 1874, the ship was sold to the U.S.

211
00:18:33,890 --> 00:18:37,640
ten years later and would see decades of service.

212
00:18:37,641 --> 00:18:45,260
with her tightly spaced reinforcing beams made of ironwood

213
00:18:45,261 --> 00:18:47,060
and metal bow plating

214
00:18:47,061 --> 00:18:50,340
she was far stronger than most ships in the water

215
00:18:50,341 --> 00:18:53,880
still she was not an icebreaker

216
00:18:53,881 --> 00:18:57,060
like the ships before her at best

217
00:18:57,061 --> 00:18:59,160
the bear was ice resistant

218
00:18:59,161 --> 00:19:02,900
having neither the power nor strength to plow through ice

219
00:19:02,901 --> 00:19:10,840
the world would have to wait another 25 years for a ship that strong

220
00:19:10,841 --> 00:19:20,560
in 1899 the first true icebreaker was built the british ship ermac purchased by the russian

221
00:19:20,561 --> 00:19:27,040
government boasted engines powerful enough to produce 10 000 horsepower but better engines

222
00:19:27,041 --> 00:19:32,640
were only part of the equation the next major step for these mighty ships was in hull design

223
00:19:32,641 --> 00:19:40,080
The Kickapoo was a unique vessel. She was built in 1921 and she was altered in 1926.

224
00:19:40,081 --> 00:19:50,320
What the Coast Guard did was to increase the length of the vessel and cut back the very bottom part of her bow, which is called the forefoot.

225
00:19:51,100 --> 00:19:57,140
And this particular design feature allows her to ride up over the ice and use the weight of the vessel to break the ice.

226
00:19:57,141 --> 00:20:02,040
This is a very important advance in icebreaker technology

227
00:20:02,041 --> 00:20:05,880
because it is really a design feature that allows the vessel

228
00:20:05,881 --> 00:20:09,660
not only to push its way through ice, but actually to break ice.

229
00:20:10,700 --> 00:20:13,820
The Northland, commissioned in 1927,

230
00:20:14,340 --> 00:20:16,520
replaced the bear in the waters off Alaska.

231
00:20:18,080 --> 00:20:20,780
Despite having sails for emergency use,

232
00:20:21,280 --> 00:20:24,020
the Northland incorporated the latest design features,

233
00:20:24,021 --> 00:20:28,920
as well as a new power plant, the recently developed diesel engine.

234
00:20:31,460 --> 00:20:36,200
The United States was now producing strong and powerful icebreaking ships,

235
00:20:36,520 --> 00:20:40,160
but their ability and importance would not be immediately recognized.

236
00:20:41,740 --> 00:20:46,800
As is often the case, the technology would have to wait for the world to catch up.

237
00:20:46,801 --> 00:21:00,040
Next, the outbreak of World War II gives icebreakers a new global purpose.

238
00:21:00,041 --> 00:21:05,200
In 1906, the famed polar explorer Roald Amundsen completed a three-year journey to become the

239
00:21:05,201 --> 00:21:09,160
first captain to sail through the elusive Northwest Passage.

240
00:21:09,161 --> 00:21:12,920
Modern icebreakers can traverse that same waterway connecting the Atlantic and Pacific

241
00:21:12,921 --> 00:21:16,137
oceans above Canada in less than three weeks.

242
00:21:16,138 --> 00:21:19,140
Icebreakers will return on Modern Marvels.

243
00:21:23,340 --> 00:21:28,260
Today, icebreakers from around the world are regular visitors to the frozen poles.

244
00:21:30,740 --> 00:21:34,560
The benefits these ships offer to science and commerce are immeasurable,

245
00:21:35,080 --> 00:21:37,880
but that has not always been recognized.

246
00:21:37,881 --> 00:21:47,220
by the late 1920s shipbuilders were making icebreakers that combined strength power and

247
00:21:47,221 --> 00:21:52,220
design features but they were expensive

248
00:21:52,221 --> 00:22:00,360
the US Coast Guard still ran the Bering Sea patrols with turn-of-the-century ships

249
00:22:00,361 --> 00:22:08,240
there seemed no reason for the newer more costly vessels but that would soon change

250
00:22:08,241 --> 00:22:14,820
in the 1930s several things happen that require the coast guard to begin ice breaking the first

251
00:22:14,821 --> 00:22:21,400
major thing that happens is that in the northeast people start heating their homes with fuel oil

252
00:22:21,401 --> 00:22:26,400
before this time they had been heating it with coal and coal was was brought by rail but heating

253
00:22:26,401 --> 00:22:29,135
oil was brought by barges up the rivers.

254
00:22:29,136 --> 00:22:32,280
The shift in consumer habits prompted a second

255
00:22:32,281 --> 00:22:39,100
major change. In 1936, President Roosevelt ordered the U.S. Coast Guard into the ice

256
00:22:39,101 --> 00:22:40,423
breaking business.

257
00:22:40,424 --> 00:22:45,420
The 1936 executive order by President Roosevelt that stated that the

258
00:22:45,421 --> 00:22:50,160
Coast Guard should try to keep the navigable waters open during winter months basically

259
00:22:50,161 --> 00:22:51,740
caught the Coast Guard unprepared.

260
00:22:55,200 --> 00:22:58,760
Their turn-of-the-century fleet would no longer suffice.

261
00:22:59,680 --> 00:23:02,960
The Coast Guard began an intensive study of icebreaker technology.

262
00:23:04,600 --> 00:23:07,160
Leading the research was Lieutenant Edward Thiel,

263
00:23:08,040 --> 00:23:11,000
who immediately turned to European designers for help.

264
00:23:12,900 --> 00:23:16,580
Because harsh Scandinavian winters would freeze harbors solid,

265
00:23:17,580 --> 00:23:21,860
Countries such as Denmark and Sweden built some of the best icebreakers in the world.

266
00:23:24,920 --> 00:23:29,740
Thiel found that many of the European ships utilized biopropellers to clear the way in front of them,

267
00:23:30,200 --> 00:23:34,460
while powerful diesel-electric engines battered the unrelenting ice.

268
00:23:36,740 --> 00:23:41,800
When he was over in Europe and looking at the icebreakers of the northern European countries,

269
00:23:41,801 --> 00:23:48,620
One of the things that Thiel realized was that some of the technology was already in use in the United States in other vessels.

270
00:23:48,880 --> 00:23:53,400
For example, the bow thrusters were being used on Great Lakes car ferries.

271
00:23:53,560 --> 00:23:58,880
What he was surprised is that nobody had put all of this technology together to create or to build an icebreaker.

272
00:24:00,380 --> 00:24:06,200
The culmination of Thiel's efforts was the 110-foot Raritan-class icebreaker Tugs.

273
00:24:07,340 --> 00:24:09,760
Four of these vessels would be built in 1939.

274
00:24:09,761 --> 00:24:16,060
Their relatively short length allowed the ships to follow the winding leads and cracks in the ice.

275
00:24:16,620 --> 00:24:19,840
But they still had plenty of power to punch through when necessary.

276
00:24:21,020 --> 00:24:25,740
But Thiel's greatest contribution to icebreakers would come as the world spiraled into war.

277
00:24:33,680 --> 00:24:38,760
With the outbreak of World War II, the icebreakers' reach would become global.

278
00:24:39,760 --> 00:24:43,100
a new breed of ship would become vital in the frozen North Atlantic.

279
00:24:44,560 --> 00:24:48,620
To remain for long periods of time in ice fields above the polar circle,

280
00:24:49,500 --> 00:24:52,060
these ships needed to be both phenomenally strong

281
00:24:52,061 --> 00:24:55,060
and large enough to carry the necessary supplies.

282
00:24:56,880 --> 00:25:02,700
The 269-foot wind-class icebreakers were contracted in November of 1941.

283
00:25:04,140 --> 00:25:07,140
Some of the world's first true polar icebreakers,

284
00:25:07,800 --> 00:25:10,940
the ships were strengthened by their close spacing of internal frames

285
00:25:10,941 --> 00:25:12,660
and carefully designed trusses.

286
00:25:13,760 --> 00:25:17,160
Combined with the 1 and 5 eighths inch welded steel hull plating,

287
00:25:18,060 --> 00:25:22,960
these ships could resist 3,000 pounds of pressure per square inch along the waterline.

288
00:25:24,720 --> 00:25:29,900
At the beginning of the war, we based our designs on the breakers that they had in Europe.

289
00:25:30,520 --> 00:25:34,080
They were much smaller icebreakers, and about 75% of them had a bow screw.

290
00:25:34,081 --> 00:25:40,080
We figured that the Europeans never left the fjords over there because as soon as we went into hard Arctic ice, we broke screws.

291
00:25:40,081 --> 00:25:46,080
And consequently, they were built that way, but we took them off within a couple of years.

292
00:25:48,080 --> 00:25:53,080
Once they were redesigned, the wind class breakers became the best the world had ever seen.

293
00:25:53,081 --> 00:25:57,080
So good that none fought for the U.S.

294
00:25:57,780 --> 00:26:03,240
The North Wind, the South Wind, and the West Wind were loaned to the Russians for the duration of the war

295
00:26:03,241 --> 00:26:08,060
to help keep their ports open, which were very important for supplying the Russian war machine.

296
00:26:09,900 --> 00:26:11,480
But there were other fronts.

297
00:26:12,640 --> 00:26:15,740
With its best icebreaking ship serving in Russian waters,

298
00:26:16,460 --> 00:26:19,640
the United States called on the breakers of the Bering Sea Patrol

299
00:26:19,641 --> 00:26:23,180
to help in the strategically important waters of the North Atlantic.

300
00:26:23,181 --> 00:26:30,180
These ships as well as others would form the Greenland Patrol.

301
00:26:38,180 --> 00:26:45,180
On patrol in the Arctic off Greenland, the United States Coast Guard Cutter moves through bleak iceberg infested waters.

302
00:26:45,181 --> 00:26:52,180
She is one of four cutters on a two months mission to smash German fortified radio bases on the remote Greenland coasts.

303
00:26:52,181 --> 00:26:58,300
One of the most important fronts during World War II for the United States was the Greenland Theater of Operations.

304
00:26:58,700 --> 00:27:01,220
Many people don't realize how important that was.

305
00:27:01,580 --> 00:27:06,980
Greenland was being used by the Germans to predict the weather over the European Theater.

306
00:27:07,380 --> 00:27:11,600
They were sending weather teams there to send out the weather reports.

307
00:27:12,880 --> 00:27:17,140
The other strategic need for Greenland was the cryolite mines.

308
00:27:17,141 --> 00:27:22,500
and acryolite is a very important mineral that is used in the production of aluminum.

309
00:27:23,620 --> 00:27:27,040
Throughout the war, the ships and crews of the Greenland Patrol

310
00:27:27,041 --> 00:27:29,640
would attack and destroy German fortifications,

311
00:27:30,480 --> 00:27:34,480
thwarting their enemies' best efforts to acquire vital information and resources.

312
00:27:36,440 --> 00:27:40,160
At the end of the war, it seemed the ships, like the sailors who manned them,

313
00:27:40,740 --> 00:27:43,900
would return to the peacetime business of clearing shipping lanes.

314
00:27:43,901 --> 00:27:47,500
But that was not to be.

315
00:27:50,860 --> 00:27:57,500
Next, new global tensions leave the battle-tested American icebreakers in the coldest part of the Cold War.

316
00:27:59,740 --> 00:28:06,500
While the Soviet Union unleashes the power of the atom to build the strongest icebreakers the world has ever seen.

317
00:28:06,501 --> 00:28:20,501
After serving over 20 years as an icebreaker, the 216-foot cutter Northland was sold to the Israeli Navy, making her the largest warship in their fleet.

318
00:28:23,520 --> 00:28:25,940
Icebreakers will return on Modern Marvels.

319
00:28:25,941 --> 00:28:33,616
In December of 1946, Operation High Jump became the first post-war U.S.

320
00:28:33,617 --> 00:28:37,400
military mission to the South Pole.

321
00:28:39,500 --> 00:28:43,624
Commanded by Admiral Richard E. Byrd, 12 ships, including the U.S.

322
00:28:43,625 --> 00:28:49,560
Coast Guard's North Wind and the Navy icebreaker Burton Island, were to make way to Antarctica.

323
00:28:49,561 --> 00:28:57,860
The convoy's mission was to build the outpost Little America 4 near the Bay of Wales.

324
00:28:59,080 --> 00:29:04,480
Unfortunately, the 269-foot Burton Island, which was similar in design to the North Wind,

325
00:29:05,060 --> 00:29:07,260
was not built in time to join the convoy.

326
00:29:08,860 --> 00:29:12,280
This left the North Wind in charge of all ice-breaking duties

327
00:29:12,281 --> 00:29:15,180
and would have nearly disastrous consequences.

328
00:29:15,181 --> 00:29:21,380
At different times during the mission, all 11 vessels became trapped in ice.

329
00:29:22,320 --> 00:29:25,920
The first ship to become beset was the USS Mount Olympus.

330
00:29:26,800 --> 00:29:34,340
The Northwind made use of its towing rigs at the rear of the ship to pull the Navy frigate close and drag it from the clutching ice.

331
00:29:36,660 --> 00:29:44,180
Another ship in the convoy, the USS Yancey, had her three-quarter-inch thick steel hull pierced by a windblown ice mass.

332
00:29:45,180 --> 00:29:47,600
She also had to be towed to safety.

333
00:29:50,920 --> 00:29:56,540
Equally unlucky, the submarine's Senate was so severely damaged after colliding with the ice,

334
00:29:57,120 --> 00:29:59,380
she was forced to abandon the mission completely.

335
00:30:03,780 --> 00:30:08,120
The remaining ships pushed forward, following the Northwind's lead

336
00:30:08,121 --> 00:30:12,300
as it opened the Bay of Wales to be used as a port and staging area.

337
00:30:12,301 --> 00:30:22,280
We first went to Antarctica, of course, in 1946, and then that kind of slacked off until the early 50s, and they started what they called Operation Deep Freeze.

338
00:30:23,720 --> 00:30:32,840
Every year since 1955, Operation Deep Freeze has sent a convoy of ships to the South Pole for base resupply and other military purposes.

339
00:30:35,420 --> 00:30:39,160
And every year, an icebreaker contingent has led the way.

340
00:30:42,300 --> 00:30:49,320
But the North Pole, due to its close proximity to the Soviet Union, would become far more strategically important.

341
00:30:50,980 --> 00:30:58,360
Fearing a Russian sneak attack, the United States built multiple distant early warning radar sites in northern Canada and Greenland.

342
00:30:59,960 --> 00:31:04,480
Known as the Dew Line, these sites would need constant resupply.

343
00:31:05,440 --> 00:31:07,900
Again, the icebreakers would lead the way.

344
00:31:07,901 --> 00:31:13,660
The Soviet Union also found the frozen polar waters extremely important.

345
00:31:17,660 --> 00:31:22,280
Mariners had long dreamed of opening northern shipping lanes like the Northwest Passage,

346
00:31:22,760 --> 00:31:25,360
connecting the Pacific and Atlantic Ocean above Canada.

347
00:31:26,720 --> 00:31:30,900
For the Russians, the Great Northern Sea Route was the goal.

348
00:31:32,740 --> 00:31:37,800
In hopes of finding a way through the treacherous ice fields above northern Europe and Asia,

349
00:31:37,900 --> 00:31:42,780
Russian icebreakers were sent on missions in the early 1950s.

350
00:31:46,520 --> 00:31:49,780
Researchers and scientists would study atmospheric,

351
00:31:51,940 --> 00:31:52,640
water,

352
00:31:54,040 --> 00:31:56,420
and ice conditions.

353
00:32:00,020 --> 00:32:05,020
The information gathered would help them push icebreaker design to a new level.

354
00:32:05,021 --> 00:32:10,620
In 1959, the icebreaker Lenin was built.

355
00:32:12,220 --> 00:32:15,980
She would be the first surface vessel powered by nuclear reactors.

356
00:32:17,120 --> 00:32:20,080
On her first trial run in May of 1960,

357
00:32:21,260 --> 00:32:24,620
a contingent of shipbuilders and scientists were on hand

358
00:32:24,621 --> 00:32:28,180
to test everything from ice strength

359
00:32:28,181 --> 00:32:33,780
to radioactive hazards.

360
00:32:35,020 --> 00:32:38,960
The Lenin would become so successful in the waters off northern Siberia

361
00:32:38,961 --> 00:32:42,900
that she would be called on when other icebreakers were beset.

362
00:32:46,960 --> 00:32:51,060
The Soviet Union, with its abundance of untapped northern resources,

363
00:32:51,860 --> 00:32:55,480
would organize the greatest icebreaker missions the world has ever seen.

364
00:32:58,380 --> 00:33:03,860
Transportation of ore, oil, and countless other materials would continue year-round.

365
00:33:05,020 --> 00:33:12,820
To achieve this in the frozen northern waters, the Soviets would continue to build nuclear

366
00:33:12,821 --> 00:33:17,200
icebreakers through the 60s and 70s.

367
00:33:17,201 --> 00:33:26,580
They would become the fastest and strongest icebreakers of all time, able to force through

368
00:33:26,581 --> 00:33:30,820
eight feet of ice at 10 knots per hour.

369
00:33:30,821 --> 00:33:37,820
For the United States, the ships and missions would also begin to change.

370
00:33:37,821 --> 00:33:40,820
In the late 1960s, the Coast Guard took over several Navy icebreakers

371
00:33:40,821 --> 00:33:44,820
and we became the sole operators of U.S. polar icebreakers.

372
00:33:44,821 --> 00:33:47,820
The mission began to change as well, particularly in the Arctic,

373
00:33:47,821 --> 00:33:51,820
toward meeting the needs of science.

374
00:33:51,821 --> 00:33:56,820
But the aging, wind-class icebreakers with their space-consuming deck guns

375
00:33:56,821 --> 00:34:00,320
were better suited for war than scientific exploration.

376
00:34:01,840 --> 00:34:06,080
In the late 1960s, partially due to the changing mission

377
00:34:06,081 --> 00:34:09,040
and partially due to superior Soviet ships,

378
00:34:09,580 --> 00:34:12,340
the U.S. began research on a new class of icebreaker,

379
00:34:13,200 --> 00:34:14,200
the Polar Class.

380
00:34:15,100 --> 00:34:18,260
The Polar Class, the Polar Sea and the Polar Star,

381
00:34:18,420 --> 00:34:21,540
which were built in the mid-'70s,

382
00:34:21,940 --> 00:34:25,300
were a substantial performance improvement over the wind class,

383
00:34:25,301 --> 00:34:30,120
predominantly because of the much larger size and much greater horsepower.

384
00:34:31,120 --> 00:34:34,620
The Polar class is able to run in two power ranges.

385
00:34:35,660 --> 00:34:40,740
The economically efficient diesel-electric mode can produce a total of 18,000 horsepower.

386
00:34:42,980 --> 00:34:47,400
But if necessary, the captain can employ the gas turbines.

387
00:34:48,140 --> 00:34:53,060
Able to create 75,000 horsepower, these engines are so forceful,

388
00:34:53,061 --> 00:34:55,320
They can only be used for short bursts.

389
00:34:57,020 --> 00:35:02,500
The 399-foot polar vessels became the most capable breakers in the U.S. fleet.

390
00:35:03,160 --> 00:35:07,280
By comparison, usually in McMurdo Base down in Antarctica,

391
00:35:07,880 --> 00:35:10,280
to break through the ice to take the supply ships in,

392
00:35:11,040 --> 00:35:14,080
you have 150, maybe 300 miles of ice to break.

393
00:35:14,920 --> 00:35:18,220
The old wind class used to take them two to three weeks.

394
00:35:19,180 --> 00:35:23,180
The last I heard the record was by the polar class was 17 hours.

395
00:35:23,800 --> 00:35:26,300
I mean, when they go to break, they break.

396
00:35:28,060 --> 00:35:31,160
Other advancements were also included in the polar ships.

397
00:35:32,080 --> 00:35:34,480
Their bows extend far out over the water.

398
00:35:35,420 --> 00:35:37,640
This transfers additional weight onto the ice.

399
00:35:38,800 --> 00:35:43,300
While at the back of the ship, the propellers were built so their pitch could be controlled.

400
00:35:43,301 --> 00:35:51,580
What the controllable pitch propellers allows you to do is to continually rotate the shaft in the same direction

401
00:35:51,581 --> 00:35:58,100
and yet change your thrust from a head thrust to a stern thrust by changing the pitch on the propeller blades.

402
00:35:59,660 --> 00:36:05,700
This advantage allows the ship to change direction without first stopping, then reversing the engines.

403
00:36:07,140 --> 00:36:09,760
But even the new ships had room for improvements.

404
00:36:09,761 --> 00:36:15,760
Having been designed as both science ships and military supply vessels,

405
00:36:15,761 --> 00:36:19,760
sacrifices have been made to accommodate both roles.

406
00:36:19,761 --> 00:36:25,760
The Coast Guard realized that the ships had some shortcomings with regard to laboratory space

407
00:36:25,761 --> 00:36:29,760
and over-the-side equipment such as oceanographic winches.

408
00:36:29,761 --> 00:36:35,760
So in the 80s, we designed a number of very significant ship alterations to the polar class,

409
00:36:35,761 --> 00:36:40,020
which added a much more robust scientific capability.

410
00:36:43,080 --> 00:36:47,940
The end of the Cold War saw an almost complete change in the U.S. icebreaker fleet.

411
00:36:49,600 --> 00:36:52,460
They were more than just ships that could break ice.

412
00:36:53,360 --> 00:36:56,500
They had become high-latitude research vessels.

413
00:36:56,501 --> 00:37:06,360
Russia would also see a change in their ships, but not for the better.

414
00:37:07,400 --> 00:37:13,720
The crumbling post-Cold War economy would offer little financial support for the once grand nuclear fleet.

415
00:37:15,000 --> 00:37:17,760
Today, a handful of the ships continue to operate,

416
00:37:17,761 --> 00:37:24,980
but many questions regarding safety and stability reverberate through the international maritime community.

417
00:37:26,500 --> 00:37:28,660
No, no, no, no.

418
00:37:30,020 --> 00:37:33,560
No, it's measuring. It's going up to 1,000. That's very dangerous.

419
00:37:36,660 --> 00:37:43,380
Of primary concern, the radioactive hearts of these vessels and storage of spent fuel,

420
00:37:43,900 --> 00:37:49,540
leaving some to believe the ships are nuclear disasters waiting to happen.

421
00:37:49,541 --> 00:37:58,380
Next, the quest for polar science advances

422
00:37:58,381 --> 00:38:03,100
as the U.S. Coast Guard unveils the newest icebreaker in its fleet.

423
00:38:06,440 --> 00:38:12,420
On August 17, 1977, the nuclear-powered Soviet icebreaker, Arctica,

424
00:38:12,880 --> 00:38:16,120
became the first surface vessel to break through to the North Pole.

425
00:38:16,121 --> 00:38:19,640
Icebreakers will return on Modern Marvelous.

426
00:38:22,540 --> 00:38:27,280
The newest member of the Coast Guard icebreaker fleet was commissioned in 1999.

427
00:38:29,940 --> 00:38:35,320
The U.S. Coast Guard cutter Healy is the largest non-nuclear icebreaker ever built.

428
00:38:36,940 --> 00:38:40,080
Constructed by Avondale Industries Incorporated in New Orleans,

429
00:38:40,081 --> 00:38:48,640
The 420-foot, 16,000-ton Healey boasts engines capable of producing 30,000 horsepower.

430
00:38:51,040 --> 00:38:54,340
Though less powerful than her peers in the polar class,

431
00:38:55,060 --> 00:38:58,580
the Healey has been designed to do far more with fewer resources.

432
00:39:00,720 --> 00:39:03,820
Sophisticated computer control systems mean, if necessary,

433
00:39:04,280 --> 00:39:07,420
the entire ship can be sailed by as few as four people.

434
00:39:07,421 --> 00:39:11,440
two on the bridge attending to navigation

435
00:39:11,441 --> 00:39:15,280
and two in the engine control center

436
00:39:15,281 --> 00:39:20,180
monitoring and maintaining the enormous power plant and drive systems

437
00:39:20,181 --> 00:39:26,500
but a much larger crew is needed for polar missions

438
00:39:26,501 --> 00:39:30,240
the Healy can accommodate 75 sailors

439
00:39:30,241 --> 00:39:32,560
and 50 science specialists

440
00:39:32,561 --> 00:39:37,660
from barbershop to onboard medical center

441
00:39:37,661 --> 00:39:42,240
the Healy must provide for every need during the long months at sea

442
00:39:42,241 --> 00:39:50,240
the Healy also houses some very special passengers

443
00:39:50,241 --> 00:39:55,860
two Dolphin H-65 helicopters can be hangered on the mid-deck

444
00:39:55,861 --> 00:39:59,140
and used for reconnaissance and transportation

445
00:39:59,141 --> 00:40:07,140
In addition to the onboard equipment, the Healy has other attributes that make her remarkable.

446
00:40:07,141 --> 00:40:13,140
The Healy has a much more squared off hull form than the Polar class.

447
00:40:13,141 --> 00:40:21,140
Icebreakers in general have a reputation for rolling very badly in a seaway because of the football shape of the hull.

448
00:40:21,141 --> 00:40:27,140
Healy on the other hand has proven to be a very nice sea boat and rides very well in a seaway.

449
00:40:29,140 --> 00:40:34,140
The Healy is at home in the water, as was her namesake.

450
00:40:34,141 --> 00:40:41,140
Captain Michael Healy gained notoriety as the hard-working, hard-drinking captain of

451
00:40:41,141 --> 00:40:46,140
the U.S. revenue cutter Bear during the early years of the Bering Sea Patrol.

452
00:40:46,141 --> 00:40:53,140
Captain Healy was a very capable seaman, very renowned for his knowledge of the ice

453
00:40:53,141 --> 00:40:56,140
and ice handling, but a very tough disciplinarian.

454
00:40:56,141 --> 00:40:58,080
Captain Healy's nickname was Hellroar and Mike.

455
00:40:58,340 --> 00:41:02,000
He was certainly not a shy or shrinking person,

456
00:41:02,160 --> 00:41:04,460
but probably the most experienced revenue cutter captain.

457
00:41:06,180 --> 00:41:08,700
As if to proclaim her wild heritage,

458
00:41:09,240 --> 00:41:12,400
during her launching ceremony, the Healy dropped into the water

459
00:41:12,401 --> 00:41:15,040
and raised a 30-foot wave,

460
00:41:15,200 --> 00:41:19,040
which washed over spectators and dignitaries in the grandstand.

461
00:41:22,800 --> 00:41:24,860
Like the captain she was named after,

462
00:41:25,460 --> 00:41:29,300
she seemed a little too eager to leave the comforts of the warm southern waters

463
00:41:29,301 --> 00:41:32,020
and head for the frozen polar north.

464
00:41:34,720 --> 00:41:38,420
Her first encounter with ice was during an eight-month trial period

465
00:41:38,421 --> 00:41:40,720
in the waters between Canada and Greenland.

466
00:41:44,200 --> 00:41:48,120
The Healy's performance pleasantly surprised the captain and crew.

467
00:41:49,540 --> 00:41:52,820
During the ice trials, we wanted to test the ship, first of all, in level ice.

468
00:41:52,821 --> 00:42:01,220
We did that in several thicknesses, and we found that while the design criteria specified that the ship must break at least 4 1⁄2 feet of ice continuously at 3 knots,

469
00:42:01,680 --> 00:42:05,180
we found that we were able to break 5 1⁄2 feet at just under 3 knots.

470
00:42:05,240 --> 00:42:09,580
So we felt that the ship really not only met the design criteria, but exceeded them as well.

471
00:42:11,720 --> 00:42:14,560
The level ice test was a resounding success.

472
00:42:15,220 --> 00:42:20,220
But it was important to see how well the new ship would handle the tough polar pressure ridges,

473
00:42:20,221 --> 00:42:23,600
which most icebreaker crews do their best to avoid.

474
00:42:25,780 --> 00:42:30,520
Well, I think we broke a pressure rate during the test that was approximately 28 feet high,

475
00:42:30,760 --> 00:42:34,900
but it was considerably thicker than that down below the waterline where you can't see it.

476
00:42:36,300 --> 00:42:41,660
The Healy's incredible strength is tempered by her sophisticated scientific capabilities.

477
00:42:44,300 --> 00:42:48,160
Well, Healy is very well suited for a wide range of science disciplines.

478
00:42:48,161 --> 00:42:52,140
We have lots of over-the-side capability where we can take samples from the ocean bottom.

479
00:42:52,300 --> 00:42:53,900
We can take samples from the water column.

480
00:42:54,480 --> 00:42:57,280
We also have a number of electronic and acoustic systems.

481
00:42:57,400 --> 00:43:01,540
We have a bottom mapping sonar where we can actually map the topography of the ocean bottom.

482
00:43:02,520 --> 00:43:07,860
We also have things like a bow tower so we can do meteorological research, atmospheric research.

483
00:43:08,260 --> 00:43:14,520
Pretty much anything in biology, geology, oceanography, all of those things are capabilities of the vessel.

484
00:43:18,160 --> 00:43:27,160
In years past, captains used to look out at the ice in front of their ship and see a barrier

485
00:43:27,161 --> 00:43:29,579
that could not be crossed.

486
00:43:29,580 --> 00:43:35,160
Today, they see a door to discovery, to which the icebreaker

487
00:43:35,161 --> 00:43:36,470
is the key.

488
00:43:36,471 --> 00:43:45,160
The future of these magnificent ships will involve a complicated marriage

489
00:43:45,161 --> 00:43:48,720
between shipbuilders and polar scientists,

490
00:43:49,440 --> 00:43:53,180
making icebreakers not only the toughest ships on the water,

491
00:43:54,020 --> 00:43:57,080
but also some of the smartest.

